okay today I'm going to talk to you  about specific latent heat so specific  latent heat is basically all about the  amount of thermal energy required in  order to change state so remember what  changing state either means is melting  boiling condensing or freezing so let's  just have a quick recap about what's  happening on a kind of atomic level when  we're talking about change of state so  let's say here I've got a substance  these these are different molecules of  that particular substance between those  molecules between those molecules there  are these forces that are called inter  molecular bonds so these are bonds in  between these different molecules in  order let's say this was liquid here in  order for this liquid to turn from a  liquid to a gas I'd need to break these  bonds and energy is required to break  these bonds so I need to heat it up put  thermal energy in and break these  intermolecular bonds so this can turn  from a liquid to a gas  so there's actually an experiment that  we can do and the results really help us  visualize what's happening so what we  can do is we if we get a Bunsen burner  here let's say and on the Bunsen burner  I've got a beaker and within the beaker  I have lots of ice what I can do is I  can actually heat up this heat up the  ice and then read my temperature change  on a thermite er so I'm also going to  need a stop clock right here so here's  my stop clock so what I'm going to do is  I'm going to start heating up my my ice  in my beaker and the second of you I  start my stopwatch then what I do is  every 20 seconds I'm going to record the  temperature on my thermometer  and I should be able to plot my data on  a graph now this is what your data  should look like roughly from this  experiment  so you'd plot temperature on the y-axis  and your time on the x-axis what you  should find is over time it starts to  rise up here now at the beginning in  fact I draw the shape first so this is  what we should find it should look  something like this now let me explain  what we're looking at here now this is  the temperature of the ice at the  beginning of the experiment okay so this  is my temperature of I of my ice which  is obviously a solid so when I'm heating  up the solid there's more energy is  going into the kinetics tour of these of  these particles because they are  vibrating more the more energy we give  them the more that they vibrate  therefore the temperature increases now  this is the bit it's a little bit  strange as the ice starts to melt we do  not have a temperature rise the reason  for that is all of the energy is going  into breaking the intermolecular bonds  within the ice so therefore we get this  flat section here where the temperature  does not go up because all of that  energy is going into breaking but  breaking bonds instead now once all of  that has melted we then have an increase  in temperature again this is when we  have water because it's all melted it's  all now a liquid so again we have an  increase in temperature here because now  it's water liquid we're again just  putting more energy into the kinetic  store or the thermal store of the water  now when we get to this point here again  we have another flat section this is  when boiling has happened again we're  not having a temperature rise because  all of that energy is going into  breaking the intermolecular bonds  between the water molecules in order to  turn it into a gas  so this is when boiling is happening and  finally this section shows steam which  is obviously a gas so what specific  latent heat actually means it's the  amount of energy required in order to  get one kilogram of a substance so one  kilogram of a substance to change to  change state without an increase in  temperature so it's basically we're  thinking about these flat portions of  the graph here that is what specific  latent heat is all about so yeah and the  next thing we need to know is we just  need to know the equation associated  with specific latent heat and how to use  it so here it is right here Q equals M  times L and here's an equation triangle  that kind of shows that as well so Q  stands for thermal energy so this is the  thermal energy that would be putting in  in order to change state the M is simply  the mass of the liquid that we're  changing which is measured in kilograms  and finally L that's substantial a  specific latent heat specific latent  heat and that units specific specific  latent heat is joules per kilogram so  let's just imagine this let's say that  we're told the specific latent heat for  water is three hundred and thirty-four  joules per kilogram you don't need to  know this number you'd always be given a  specific latent heat your question might  be how much thermal energy is required  in order to change the state of 500  grams of of water and then you'd be  given this value here so all you'd need  to do is use this equation now please  notice this is in grams we need to  convert to kilograms so you'd have to do  this 500 divided by a thousand which  equals 0.5 so to find the specific  latent heat of this substance  you'd simply do 0.5 x 3 3 4 and let's  see what that would give you 167 so the  amount of thermal energy required in  order to change 500 grams change the  state of 500 grams of water would be a  hundred and sixty-seven joules so the  final thing we need to know is just two  more terms so there's two different  types of a specific latent heat if we're  talking about either melting or freezing  this is called the specific latent heat  of fusion and if we're thinking about  boiling or condensing this is called the  specific latent heat of vaporization and  a final final point a lot of people get  specific latent heat and procedure  specific heat capacity confused a nice  little way to try and remember which  ones which  is latent has got eight in it and so  does state so this one is about changing  state and that is about it thank you
 

